  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 PM EDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nA stagnant rex block pattern continues across the Great Lakes and \nOhio Valley today. Water vapor imagery early this afternoon indicates\ntwo distinct short waves progressing through this pattern with the\nfirst dropping southeast across far southern Indiana/western Kentucky\nand another vort max drifting westward through the base of the northern\nlatitude ridge across southern Lake Michigan/SE Wisconsin. There appears\nto be two distinct mid level height minima with this pattern, with\na tendency of some weak phasing through the afternoon and early\nevening hours. This should result in some continued respectable low\nlevel moisture transport from east to west across northern Indiana\nand northwest Ohio through this evening. Some subtle increase \nin moisture depth paired with resultant 250-500 J/kg of MUCAPE \ncould give rise to some isolated to scattered showers mainly \nalong and east of I-69 across NE Indiana/NW Ohio through this \nevening. To this point, much of the precipitation has been in \nthe form of some patchy drizzle, and this should continue to be \nthe case this evening for western/central areas as the southern \nLake MI vort max begins to shift southward.\n\nBy late tonight into Thursday AM, easterly low level flow should\nbegin to advect drier air westward to the local area as the \nOhio Valley negative mid/upper level height anomaly begins to \nbecome more suppressed southward. This should open the door for \nsome decreasing low clouds across northern half later tonight \ninto Thursday, but uncertainty persists with cloud cover during \nthe day Thursday. Guidance would suggest best chance of low \nclouds lingering into Thursday afternoon would be across western\nand southern portions of the forecast area. Some low level \nthermal modification and likelihood of some decrease in clouds \nshould allow highs to be 4 to 8 degrees warmer tomorrow, but a \nlower than normal confidence exists with temps tomorrow based on\nthe potential of that lingering low cloud cover.\n\nOverall, very few changes are warranted for this forecast package \nfor Friday and beyond with a relatively stable longwave pattern \nremaining in place through the weekend. This stable wave pattern \ninvolves preference for a central CONUS ridge, with primary \nnegative upper height anomalies across southwest Canada and New \nEngland. A couple of upper level short waves are expected to \neject northeast from the Pacific NW/southwest Canadian upper \ntrough into the Northern Plains/Upper Midwest Friday, and then \nagain late weekend, but persistence of the stable longwave \npattern and background mean upper ridging should result in \nlittle in the way of notable mid/upper level forcing through the\nfirst part of next week as these short waves dampen upstream. \nTemperatures are expected to remain below normal through the \nweekend in this stagnant pattern, with an opportunity for cooler\nnights in the mid-upper 40s Thu night through Sat night due to \nless cloud cover. \n\nPresent indications still suggest the stable wave pattern breaks \ndown Tuesday and beyond with more progression in the flow \ndeveloping, and the potential of more dynamic eastern Pacific waves \nreaching the Upper Midwest/Great Lakes region. This likely will \nrepresent the next notable chance of showers/embedded storms with a \npotential larger scale frontal response possible by next Tue or\nWed.",
